  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWeak cold front will continue to push across the state through\ntonight. As a result cooler temperatures will return tonight,\nwith a steady north wind. Given track of a surface high tonight\nand clear skies, blended in NBM 10th percentile. Near to \nslightly below freezing temperatures will be found for some \nareas, mainly in the northwest closer to the surface high. \nHumidity values may also increased tonight. Some patchy fog is \npossible, especially in the north and west where winds are \nexpected to go light from the mentioned surface high. Confidence\nwas not high enough to include in the forecast at this time. \nWeak ridging builds back in Wednesday, while the surface high \nmoves east. Behind tonight's front could be slightly cooler, \nyet still above normal, highs for Wednesday in the 60s. As the \nsurface high moves east winds will become easterly to southerly.\nGiven this mixing blended NBM with NBM 90th percentile which \nbrings slightly higher temperatures. Mainly dry and clear \nconditions are also expected for Wednesday. A steady south wind \nand southwest flow aloft will bring mild and dry conditions \nWednesday night. Look for lows in the 40s. Increased dewpoints \nfrom this south wind could also bring some higher RH values \nWednesday night. Perhaps more patchy fog is possible then, \nalthough the mentioned increased winds and overall dry state of\nthe atmosphere should limit this threat.\n\nThursday and Friday see southwest flow aloft ahead of a larger\nupper level wave, and developing surface low. Expect very warm\nconditions from this pattern both Thursday and Friday. Given\nample mixing from southerly flow, continued blending in NBM \nwith NBM90th for high temperatures. This brings highs in the 70s\nand low 80s each day. Some sites with highs near 80 would\napproach record highs for this time of year. ECMWF EFI values\nfor MaxT on Friday are elevated showing this potential. Surface\nlow encroaches on the state Friday which could make for some\nbreezier southerly winds. Very warm temperatures could lower RH\nvalues enough for some near critical fire weather conditions\nwith these breezy southerly winds. Increasing RH is the limiting\nfactor for this potential thus will continue to monitor. Lifting\nwarm front with this surface low could also bring some isolated\nshowers and perhaps thunderstorms Friday. CSU-MLP showing some\nlow probability for isolated severe storms with this front.\nShear looks adequate for severe weather, although instability is\nlimited.\n\nStrong cold front then moves through sometime later Friday or\nearly Saturday. This could bring another isolated to scattered\nchances for showers and thunderstorms. Much cooler temperatures\nand breezy to windy conditions will then be found for Saturday.\nPerhaps some fire weather concerns will linger into Saturday,\nalthough cooler temperatures should help limit this. Cooler\ntemperatures then look to remain Saturday nigh with lows in the\n20s. Sunday remains cool with less wind. Expect dry conditions\nas well on Sunday.",
